name: job control
boss: BackgroundNoise
flavor: I keep running after you look away.
brief: A `&` at the end runs a command in the background and hands you back the
brief: prompt. `jobs` lists what you started, `fg` brings one forward, `bg` resumes a
brief: stopped one. `^Z` stops the job in front of you.
brief:
brief: `$!` is the pid of the last thing you backgrounded. `wait` blocks until it
brief: finishes. That pair is how a script starts several things at once and
brief: then collects them.
brief:
brief: Backgrounded is not the same as safe. `nohup` and `setsid` cut a command
brief: loose from the terminal that started it, so it survives the hangup.
brief: `timeout` puts a clock on one. `ulimit` caps what it may take before it
brief: takes the machine with it. `kill` ends it.
brief:
brief: All of this dies when the terminal does -- which is what `abduco` is for.
---
q: {run "sleep 2" in the background|background a sleep 2 command|start sleep 2 without blocking your prompt}
a: sleep 2 &
why: interactive
ref: sleep
teach: sleep
hint: an ampersand at the end
---
q: {run "sleep 2" in the background and print the pid it was given|background sleep 2, then print the pid it got|start sleep 2 in the background and show its pid}
a: sleep 2 & echo $!
out: ^[1-9][0-9]*$
ref: sleep
teach: sleep
hint: $! holds the pid of whatever you just backgrounded
---
q: {start "sleep 1" in the background and then wait for it to finish|background sleep 1, then wait for it to complete|run sleep 1 in the background, then wait until it's done}
a: sleep 1 & wait
why: interactive
ref: wait
teach: wait
hint: wait with no argument blocks until every background job finishes
---
q: {run two sleep 1 commands at once, then wait for both|background two sleep 1 commands, then wait for both to finish|run two sleep 1 commands concurrently, then wait until both finish}
a: sleep 1 & sleep 1 & wait
why: interactive
ref: wait
teach: wait
hint: wait with no argument waits for all backgrounded jobs, not just one
---
q: {wait for one specific background job by its pid|background sleep 1, then wait on just its pid|use wait with a pid to block on one particular job}
a: sleep 1 & wait $!
why: interactive
ref: wait
teach: wait
hint: passing $! to wait waits for that one job specifically
---
q: %list% the jobs running in this shell
a: jobs
why: interactive
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: jobs lists what this shell has running or stopped, not the whole system
---
q: {run @script@ so it keeps running after the terminal goes away, with nohup|use nohup so @script@ survives the terminal closing|start @script@ immune to the hangup signal, with nohup}
a: nohup ./@script@ &
why: interactive
ref: nohup
teach: nohup
hint: nohup makes the process ignore the hangup signal
---
q: {run @script@ in a new session, cut off from this terminal, with setsid|use setsid to start @script@ with no controlling terminal|start @script@ in its own session, cut off from this one, with setsid}
a: setsid ./@script@
why: interactive
ref: setsid
teach: setsid
hint: setsid starts the process in a new session with no controlling terminal at all
---
setup: a step in your script might hang forever waiting on something
q: {cap @script@ at 5 seconds with timeout, then kill it|run @script@ under a 5 second timeout|use timeout to cap @script@ at 5 seconds}
a: timeout 5 ./@script@
out: running
ref: timeout
teach: timeout
hint: timeout runs the command and sends TERM if it's still going after the limit
---
q: {cap sleep 10 at 2 seconds with timeout, force-killing it, no TERM first|run sleep 10 under a 2 second timeout that skips straight to KILL|give sleep 10 two seconds under timeout, then make sure it dies via KILL}
a: timeout -s KILL 2 sleep 10
why: interactive
ref: timeout
teach: timeout -s
hint: -s picks which signal timeout sends when the limit is hit
---
q: {give sleep 10 one second under timeout, then force-kill it if it won't die|cap sleep 10 at 1 second with timeout, escalating to KILL if it won't die|run sleep 10 under a 1 second timeout that escalates to KILL}
a: timeout -k 1 1 sleep 10
why: interactive
ref: timeout
teach: timeout -k
hint: -k gives a grace period, then escalates to KILL if the first signal didn't work
---
setup: abduco sessions here are called work and run /bin/sh.
q: {start a detached session called work running /bin/sh|use abduco -n to create work, detached, running /bin/sh|create the detached session work running /bin/sh}
a: abduco -n work /bin/sh
why: interactive
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -n
hint: -n creates the session detached, without attaching to it
---
setup: abduco sessions here are called work and run /bin/sh.
q: {start a session called work running /bin/sh and attach now|use abduco -c to create work on /bin/sh, attaching at once|create work running /bin/sh with abduco, attaching right away}
a: abduco -c work /bin/sh
why: interactive
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -c
hint: -c creates the session and attaches to it in one step
---
q: {reattach to the session called work|use abduco -a to reattach to work|attach back into the existing work session}
a: abduco -a work
why: interactive
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -a
hint: -a attaches to a session that already exists
---
setup: abduco sessions here are called work and run /bin/sh.
q: {attach to work, or create it on /bin/sh if it does not exist|use abduco -A so work is created on /bin/sh only if missing|attach to work, letting abduco create it on /bin/sh if needed}
a: abduco -A work /bin/sh
why: interactive
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -A
hint: -A attaches if the session exists, or creates it if it doesn't
---
q: {watch the session work without being able to type into it|use abduco -r to observe work without sending it input|open work read-only, with no typing allowed}
a: abduco -r work
why: interactive
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -r
hint: -r attaches read-only, so your input never reaches the session
---
q: %list% the sessions abduco is holding
a: abduco
why: interactive
ref: abduco
teach: abduco
hint: abduco with no session name lists what it's holding
---
q: {rebind the detach key to ctrl-q before attaching to work, with abduco|attach to work with abduco, using ctrl-q as the detach key|use abduco and ctrl-q as your detach key when you attach to work}
a: abduco -e ^q -a work
why: interactive
ref: abduco
teach: abduco -e
hint: -e sets the key sequence that detaches you, default is ctrl-\
---
q: %list% the jobs in this shell, with pids, after backgrounding sleep 1
a: sleep 1 & jobs -l
why: interactive
ref: jobs
teach: jobs -l
hint: -l adds the pid alongside each job's normal listing
---
q: {tell process 12345 to reload -- send it HUP by number, with kill|nudge process 12345 to reload its config, with kill|send process 12345 signal 1 (HUP) with kill, to make it reload}
a: kill -1 12345
why: interactive
ref: kill
teach: kill -1
hint: -1 is the signal number for HUP, same as -HUP
---
q: %show% every resource limit this shell is under, with ulimit
a: ulimit -a
cmp: ref
ref: ulimit
teach: ulimit -a
hint: -a prints every limit at once instead of just one
---
setup: a script's writes keep failing partway through, mysteriously
q: %show% the maximum file size this shell may create, with ulimit
a: ulimit -f
cmp: ref
ref: ulimit
teach: ulimit -f
hint: -f reports the max file size a process may write, in blocks
---
q: {bring the newest background job back in front of you|pull the last job you backgrounded into the foreground}, with fg
a: fg
why: interactive
ref: fg
teach: fg
hint: fg hands the terminal to the job, which is why it needs one to run
---
q: {let a job you stopped carry on, still in the background|restart a stopped job without taking your prompt back}, with bg
a: bg
why: interactive
ref: bg
teach: bg
hint: ^Z stops the job in front of you; bg is how you set it going again
